config BLOB_DB
	bool "Stable-id blob storage library"
	select FLASH
	select FLASH_MAP
	select CRC
	help
	  Enable the blob_db library: a stable-id blob store built on
	  Zephyr's flash_area API. See doc/layers/l1_blob_db.md for the on-flash
	  format, atomicity model, and stability contract.

if BLOB_DB

choice BLOB_DB_BACKEND
	prompt "blob_db storage backend"
	default BLOB_DB_BACKEND_UBI
	help
	  Selects the substrate blob_db stores blobs on. Both expose the same
	  PEB-addressed store interface (lib/blob_db/blob_db_store.h); the
	  on-flash layouts are NOT interchangeable, so switching an existing
	  device between them requires erasing the partition first.

config BLOB_DB_BACKEND_FLASH_AREA
	bool "Raw partition (flash_area)"
	help
	  Store directly on the raw storage partition via Zephyr's flash_area
	  API. This was the original behaviour and the former default. It is
	  faster — see app_perf/RESULTS.md, "The UBI backend" — but has no
	  wear-leveling and no bad-block handling: an interrupted write can
	  leave the part unusable until it is externally recovered.

config BLOB_DB_BACKEND_UBI
	bool "UBI volume"
	select UBI_ENABLE
	help
	  Store on a dynamic UBI volume, gaining wear-leveling and bad-block
	  handling. blob_db's in-place slot append maps onto ubi_leb_write_at().

	  The default, on the grounds that a store which survives a torn write
	  is worth more than a faster one. Measured cost on the nRF5340-DK
	  (app_perf/RESULTS.md): reads 2.5x slower, updates 1.5x, +23 KB of
	  flash. UBI's LEB->PEB indirection costs ~112 us per flash
	  transaction and nothing per byte, so transaction-heavy paths pay
	  most; the sector-erase cost that dominates writes is unchanged.

	  With the static memory backend (the UBI default) this requires
	  CONFIG_UBI_MAX_NR_OF_DATA_PEBS >= the partition's data PEB count,
	  which is (partition_size / erase_block_size) -
	  CONFIG_UBI_DEV_HDR_NR_OF_RES_PEBS. It defaults to 14, which builds
	  cleanly and then fails to attach at runtime, so every board that
	  uses this backend must set it. The pool costs 16 bytes per PEB, so
	  size it to the geometry rather than to the maximum.

endchoice

config BLOB_DB_PARTITION_LABEL
	string "Storage partition label"
	default "storage"
	help
	  Label of the fixed flash partition where blobs are stored. Must
	  refer to a fixed-partitions child node in the device tree.

config BLOB_DB_MAX_PAYLOAD_LEN
	int "Max single-slot payload length in bytes"
	default 2016 if BLOB_DB_LARGE_PAYLOADS
	default 256
	range 1 65535
	help
	  Maximum payload size accepted by blob_db_update. Slot size on flash
	  is bounded by this plus a 14-byte header.

	  The upper bound here is only the on-flash field width (val_len is
	  16-bit). The binding limit is the erase-block geometry, which is a
	  runtime property: a bucket is an append-only log, so a rebind needs
	  two slots to fit before compaction can reclaim the first. Mount
	  therefore refuses a build whose cap exceeds
	  (sector_size - 16) / 2 - 14 — about 2026 B on 4 KB sectors and
	  32746 B on 64 KB sectors.

config BLOB_DB_LARGE_PAYLOADS
	bool "Objects larger than one slot (segmented)"
	help
	  Store an object too large for one slot as K segment slots plus an index
	  slot at the object's id, committed by writing the index last. Bumps the
	  on-flash format major, so software without this option refuses such a
	  store rather than misreading an index record as data.

	  This lifts the size cap only. Partial access — blob_db_size(),
	  blob_db_read(), blob_db_write() — is always compiled in and works on
	  inline payloads whether or not this is set.

	  Off (default) is today's implementation byte for byte: no segmented
	  writes, no new code, the format major older builds already understand.

	  Note the index record is itself a single-slot payload, so the reachable
	  object size is bounded by BLOB_DB_MAX_PAYLOAD_LEN — which is why
	  enabling this raises that default from 256 to 2016. Mount reports the
	  achievable maximum and refuses a configuration that cannot reach
	  BLOB_DB_MAX_OBJECT_LEN.

if BLOB_DB_LARGE_PAYLOADS

config BLOB_DB_MAX_OBJECT_LEN
	int "Max logical object length in bytes"
	default 131072
	range BLOB_DB_MAX_PAYLOAD_LEN 16777216
	help
	  Largest payload blob_db_update() will accept. Reachability depends on
	  the geometry and on MAX_SEGMENTS; mount refuses a build that cannot
	  reach this and names the shortfall.

config BLOB_DB_MAX_SEGMENTS
	int "Max segments per object"
	default 128
	range 2 8192
	help
	  Sets the RAM cost of the feature: two segment-id tables of 8 bytes per
	  segment, so 16 B each. This is the knob that converts "how large an
	  object do I need" into bytes of .bss.

config BLOB_DB_SEGMENT_LEN
	int "Segment size in bytes (0 = derive from the geometry)"
	default 0
	range 0 65535
	help
	  0 picks sector_size / 4, clamped to what a slot can hold, which leaves
	  room for several segments plus rebind headroom in each bucket. Chunk
	  sizes that divide a bucket evenly maximise capacity but leave a full
	  bucket with no headroom, so the partition hits -ENOSPC abruptly instead
	  of degrading.

config BLOB_DB_TEST_CRASH_HOOKS
	bool "Test-only: allow a segmented write to be cut mid-sequence"
	help
	  Compiles in a stop point after each step of the segmented write and
	  delete sequences (doc/proposals/2026-08-09-large-payloads.md §6.4), so
	  a test can stop the operation dead at a chosen step and then remount —
	  the faithful equivalent of a power cut, since everything blob_db knows
	  lives on flash.

	  This exists because segmentation's crash-safety claim is that step 3
	  (writing the index record) is the single commit point: before it the
	  new object is invisible, after it the old one is unreachable, and a
	  crash anywhere leaves unreferenced segments rather than wrong data.
	  That claim is only worth as much as the test that tries to break it.

	  For tests only. Leave off in production builds — it adds a comparison
	  on the write path and exports a symbol that can corrupt a store.

endif # BLOB_DB_LARGE_PAYLOADS

config BLOB_DB_IOSTATS
	bool "Count flash operations and bytes"
	help
	  Count reads, writes and erases — operations and bytes — at the
	  storage seam, and expose them through blob_db_iostats_get(). Both
	  counts are kept because they can disagree: a change that moves fewer
	  bytes may issue more transactions, and on a serial part each
	  transaction carries a fixed command-and-address cost.

	  Intended for benchmarks and regression guards. Unlike wall-clock,
	  these numbers are deterministic, so they are meaningful on native_sim
	  — where the flash simulator models no latency at all unless
	  FLASH_SIMULATOR_SIMULATE_TIMING is set, and even then charges per
	  call rather than per byte.

	  A handful of counters and one call per store operation; leave it off
	  in production builds.

config BLOB_DB_AUTOFORMAT_ON_CORRUPT
	bool "Format when both master sectors are unreadable"
	default y
	help
	  Controls what mount does when neither master can be parsed AND the
	  sectors are not erased — bit rot, or a power loss during the very
	  first format.

	  y (development): format and carry on, so a bad first boot is not a
	  device needing manual intervention.

	  n (production): return -EIO and leave the partition untouched, so the
	  caller decides whether to discard it with blob_db_format().

	  This does NOT apply to a store written in a format this build does not
	  understand. That is always refused with -ENOTSUP and never formatted,
	  regardless of this option.

config BLOB_DB_SECTOR_BUF_SIZE
	int "Max flash sector size in bytes"
	default 4096
	range 256 65536
	help
	  Upper bound on the flash sector size blob_db supports. Two buffers
	  of this size live in .bss (single-threaded contract); mount refuses
	  a partition whose sector is larger than this. Set to at least the
	  target flash's largest sector — e.g. 65536 for the mx25r64 QSPI
	  NOR on the nRF5340-DK.

module = BLOB_DB
module-str = BLOB_DB
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # BLOB_DB
